#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092086 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a681cf28-41fa-5547-ba25-ee6a720e1a4f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Using the Sin-Cos-Tan Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "7302747a-df6d-5aa9-8118-c9e5f3b1574d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6192" :question/problem "Calculate, in degrees to the nearest degree, the solutions to the equation $5\\sin x = -7\\cos x$ for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ.$" :question/worked-solution "We divide each term of the equation by $\\cos x$ and use the identity $\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-\\frac{7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-\\frac{7\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\ 5\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-7 \\\\ 5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\tan x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\tan x = -\\dfrac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nThe length of the interval for $x$ is $2\\pi$, so we will likely require $2$ solutions. \n\nWe can immediately calculate the principal value $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx = \\arctan\\left(-\\dfrac{7}{5}\\right) \\approx -54.46\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the domain $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ.$ To get a value that's inside the domain, we add $180^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{7}{5})+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈-{54.46}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={125.54}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the other solution inside the given interval, we add $180^\\circ$ to $x_1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{125.54}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={305.54}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the solutions, rounded to the nearest degree, are $x= 126^\\circ, 306^\\circ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092087 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092252 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf1a53c0-e652-5732-9d5a-5aad004c2676" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Using the Sin-Cos-Tan Identity Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "072d7328-7c31-5d09-b2d3-91b564624b62" :question/math-academy-id "e-6196" :question/problem "Calculate, in degrees to the nearest degree, the solutions to the equation $3\\sin x + 12\\cos x = 0$ for $-180^\\circ <  x \\leq 180^\\circ.$" :question/worked-solution "We divide each term of the equation by $\\cos x$ and use the identity $\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{12\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{12\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+12 & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\tan x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-12 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can immediately calculate the principal value $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx = \\arctan(-4) \\approx -75.96^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the given domain, our first solution is $x_1 = -75.96^\\circ.$\n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we *add* $180^\\circ.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈-{75.96}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={104.04}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the solutions, rounded to the nearest degree, are $x= -76^\\circ, 104^\\circ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092252 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf1a53c0-e652-5732-9d5a-5aad004c2676" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Using the Sin-Cos-Tan Identity Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e4d0cf1-14c2-500e-971d-008846e30223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.471" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541514} {:answer/id #uuid "3364494f-9d2e-50db-a122-31501d5f03b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.457" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541531} {:answer/id #uuid "4c6481c8-0702-5d1e-b842-c197606f7969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.465" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541580} {:answer/id #uuid "5adb496f-7daf-5800-895d-7ea4cca8e70b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.671" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541602} {:answer/id #uuid "fd5d45cb-8359-596e-ac33-0198a27e0501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6.785" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3364494f-9d2e-50db-a122-31501d5f03b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.457" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541531} :answer-field/id #uuid "212054e1-df63-592d-9cbb-ae7340b54008" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50aa710d-f2c6-54ff-998e-2adb0b38d4dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-122681" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$  what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?" :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{5\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-5 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-5 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(10)=1.471\\,127\\,674…≈1.471\\,128\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places. \n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we substract $\\pi $ from the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=1.471\\,128-\\pi ≈-1.670\\,465\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places. \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈(1.471\\,128)\\cdot (-1.670\\,465)≈-2.457\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a681cf28-41fa-5547-ba25-ee6a720e1a4f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Using the Sin-Cos-Tan Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c4ee331-238e-59a4-8986-69bac9c080bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,198" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541449} {:answer/id #uuid "2198478a-0906-5c0b-a84e-218df9b6d508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,184" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541493} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9b6e35-f362-5186-9453-fac9e3e6cf34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,198" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541512} {:answer/id #uuid "579c0c58-6b75-506e-975b-78e02a08fb41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,184" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541596} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e46f32-f5c9-506e-89ac-1af46cb2bd4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19\\,148" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9e46f32-f5c9-506e-89ac-1af46cb2bd4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19\\,148" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541849} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb7b075f-829a-5526-980c-c8864059ac56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9ae048b-af0f-521f-b439-497b724959ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-21475" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-15\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-15\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{15\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{15\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-15 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-15 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =15 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{15}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{15}{4})=75.068\\,582{…}^{∘}≈{75.069}^{∘}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nSince ${75.069}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={75.069}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘}=75.068\\,582{…}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}≈255.069\\,{…}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is $75.069\\cdot 255.069≈19\\,148$\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a681cf28-41fa-5547-ba25-ee6a720e1a4f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Using the Sin-Cos-Tan Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9c233cf-a4f5-5697-8ace-9867ac69a8aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9c233cf-a4f5-5697-8ace-9867ac69a8aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541773} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5953468-d34e-5c69-b9d8-1f869d6afb92" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542096} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab1ee867-6d4f-5d52-827a-c9eca1e24c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab1ee867-6d4f-5d52-827a-c9eca1e24c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541776} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6787a67-b62e-55ec-ad5c-e2fca72c58d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d68734a8-20a2-53fe-99b5-ad01be577281" :question/math-academy-id "q-327804" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, rounded to two decimal places in radians, the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{10\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =10 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5)≈1.373\\,40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\nx≈1.373\\,40+\\pi ≈4.514\\,99\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places. \n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to two decimal places, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}≈1.37,\\,{x}_{2}≈4.51.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a681cf28-41fa-5547-ba25-ee6a720e1a4f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Using the Sin-Cos-Tan Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b02e5084-7e6e-56c1-b955-b4056bd55fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b02e5084-7e6e-56c1-b955-b4056bd55fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541785} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cd50e6e-50f5-5c03-a69a-4d4177a2f7b7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542026} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da7d0c4-8b9c-5382-b171-98582e84f260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1da7d0c4-8b9c-5382-b171-98582e84f260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541483} :answer-field/id #uuid "83ee76e0-3024-5b35-a1ab-995416a544b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "adbef047-215a-508f-b02c-be145edbac02" :question/math-academy-id "q-330502" :question/problem "In **ascending order**,  the exact solutions in radians to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092252 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf1a53c0-e652-5732-9d5a-5aad004c2676" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Using the Sin-Cos-Tan Identity Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2f8410c-e619-5fea-a62d-0b8449e17891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2f8410c-e619-5fea-a62d-0b8449e17891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541869} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea0be7f4-c3a2-5db1-ac5c-a4826dc9dbce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542084} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5adfe1c-bc62-5c77-ae44-a5c5d5e3df17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5adfe1c-bc62-5c77-ae44-a5c5d5e3df17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541909} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7547994-5b16-581b-833f-71dc1bfabf30" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95e3e644-e5ac-51bf-963d-8477ce790acd" :question/math-academy-id "q-330574" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the exact solutions in radians to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We divide each term of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\sqrt{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092252 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf1a53c0-e652-5732-9d5a-5aad004c2676" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Using the Sin-Cos-Tan Identity Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cd6d49a-b2a6-595c-9155-7163c185e7f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cd6d49a-b2a6-595c-9155-7163c185e7f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541641} :answer-field/id #uuid "b808f185-59db-56d5-ac80-a601b0dcff62" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542051} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31657d03-68dd-5d68-93b1-1413772d206e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31657d03-68dd-5d68-93b1-1413772d206e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541524} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea70cc01-b6ac-516a-ae7a-a88538965b52" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "68c441f4-bd23-51b1-bd56-c0eea3474184" :question/math-academy-id "q-330586" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the exact solutions in radians to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is inside the given domain, one solution is ${x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo find another solution in the domain, we subtract $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\pi =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a681cf28-41fa-5547-ba25-ee6a720e1a4f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Using the Sin-Cos-Tan Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1ec3adc-280b-5b79-adca-297dc98863e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091499} {:answer/id #uuid "f54dc8f4-5ccc-567d-974d-cb5b85a4b2d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091500} {:answer/id #uuid "f57ed522-c307-5f30-be46-8751eb32045c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091501} {:answer/id #uuid "12c8d5fa-780c-594d-acaf-04947ace4502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091502} {:answer/id #uuid "68455adb-0436-5c0f-8611-dd02a76547a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f57ed522-c307-5f30-be46-8751eb32045c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091501} :answer-field/id #uuid "507a51a0-2f38-5b8b-a0e1-0fe341eed3c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41fbb86c-53b6-5410-a547-305f18db5384" :question/math-academy-id "q-8828" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{2\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 5\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-2 & =0 \\\\ 5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})=0.380\\,506…≈0.380\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places. \n\nSince $0.380\\,5$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.380\\,5.$\n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=0.380\\,506…+\\pi =3.522\\,099…≈3.522\\,1\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places. \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈0.380\\,5\\cdot 3.522\\,1≈1.3\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a681cf28-41fa-5547-ba25-ee6a720e1a4f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Using the Sin-Cos-Tan Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15787c24-e618-53d4-8a29-3dc9499996c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.804" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541465} {:answer/id #uuid "581302f3-81bc-5a00-aa69-41b710ebfdfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.280" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541597} {:answer/id #uuid "59eed6a5-45fc-5d51-a620-a7b381132dca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.284" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541600} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f0c17b-c929-51a0-8a87-56cae3e64293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.842" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541908} {:answer/id #uuid "e663f0c8-fcdd-52e4-bb43-ebf22bfa305c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.824" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e663f0c8-fcdd-52e4-bb43-ebf22bfa305c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.824" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541911} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0898c05-a5e3-5618-a8b5-3c67837f18a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b7ee989-a51c-50ad-8da0-87452c898ca6" :question/math-academy-id "q-8837" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?" :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{9\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+9 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-9 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{9}{2})=-1.352\\,127\\,380\\,…≈-1.352\\,127\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places. \n\nSince $-1.352\\,127$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-1.352\\,127\\,380\\,…+\\pi =1.789\\,465\\,272\\,…≈1.789\\,465 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-1.352\\,127\\,380\\,…+2\\pi =4.931\\,057\\,926\\,…≈4.931\\,058\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈1.789\\,466\\cdot 4.931\\,058≈8.824\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092252 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf1a53c0-e652-5732-9d5a-5aad004c2676" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Using the Sin-Cos-Tan Identity Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e2e080-3c1b-5c3d-826a-c4649c776f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354704} {:answer/id #uuid "25019ab2-57d6-57f4-94d0-70955b245551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354975} {:answer/id #uuid "5087f04f-1a8d-50c3-9538-460bfde672a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355383} {:answer/id #uuid "5be543d7-dd87-5708-9c64-afae64f57bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355475} {:answer/id #uuid "65173a57-b4d1-57eb-b568-86395fef6a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65173a57-b4d1-57eb-b568-86395fef6a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355562} :answer-field/id #uuid "934b6b43-47e6-57a0-a839-d889e84f32a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40bdf0c4-a9af-5a19-bf92-60c84db76070" :question/math-academy-id "q-8876" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTo find the other solution, we subtract $\\pi $ from the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\pi =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}=-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092252 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf1a53c0-e652-5732-9d5a-5aad004c2676" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Using the Sin-Cos-Tan Identity Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0be73b99-a64d-5ea6-a3de-ea13e95952df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541448} {:answer/id #uuid "5984cd99-66ed-59db-a946-6a7ad6f52f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541599} {:answer/id #uuid "b650cd91-8c32-5c54-85ea-107b7fa6ac6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541797} {:answer/id #uuid "c13397a3-45e8-5979-90cb-b8c8474e84e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541829} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0afb86-7b21-55b8-820c-0fd7ceac79d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b650cd91-8c32-5c54-85ea-107b7fa6ac6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541797} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b82027b-2113-5a4b-a817-bf37e2e85492" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9106b848-9f77-574a-a8bb-ee8c8855bad8" :question/math-academy-id "q-8877" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo find the other solution, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$"}]}
